      </p>
      <p>
        When an American technology company funds its payroll in US Dollars (USD) to compensate engineers in Warsaw (Polish Złoty - PLN), São Paulo (Brazilian Real - BRL), Berlin (Euro - EUR), or London (British Pound - GBP), the EOR converts those USD balances into destination currencies. Instead of applying the true institutional interbank mid-market rate—the exact mid-point between wholesale buy and sell rates broadcasted by Reuters and the European Central Bank—the platform applies a proprietary exchange rate marked up by <strong>1.5% to 2.8%</strong>. Because this markup is bundled into the gross currency conversion figure rather than itemized as a separate fee, finance departments rarely detect the leak.
      </p>

